#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111843 :tutorial/content "Consider the following triangle, where the side $\\overline{AC}$ has been extended beyond the vertex $C$.\n\n![](images/6b/6be676cc487d17815701f3783e9eca71d7491a0e63d7ae9016fb65ad801e61d6.png)\n\nThe resulting angle $\\angle{BCD}$ is called the **exterior angle** at vertex $C$.\n\nExterior angles have the following properties:\n\n- An exterior angle and its corresponding interior angle form a linear pair and are therefore supplementary. Here, we have\n$\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{BCD}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{ACB}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} = 180^\\circ$\n- The measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. This is called the **exterior angles theorem**. To see why it is true, notice that since the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n$m∠A + m∠B + m∠C|= 180^{∘}; m∠A + m∠C|= 180^{∘} - m∠B_(⏟)_(Exterior angle at [math]B[/math])$.\n- At each vertex of a triangle, there are two exterior angles. They are vertical angles, and consequently they have the same measure.![](images/b4/b459ad1edd5e0fdf512d722eb55ab20ff66c281d7160077d12936a6926529aa0.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "d29b69d0-268e-57ed-9c22-289173b3c810" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2223 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111844 :tutorial/content "Yet another important fact about exterior angles is:\n\n> The sum of the measures of the exterior angles of a triangle is always equal to $360^\\circ$.\n\nSo, for the triangle below, we have\n\n$$\nx+y+z = 360^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/22/22586791e1118a38a554deffaec3ac5b73eafc47eee4be2052c0949dde584cea.png)\n\nTo see why, first recall that the measure of an exterior angle is equal to the sum of the measures of the two non-adjacent interior angles. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= m∠B + m∠C \\\\\ny &= m∠A + m∠C \\\\\nz &= m∠A + m∠B\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that the internal angles of a triangle sum to $180^\\circ$. Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y + z &= (m∠B + m∠C) + (m∠A + m∠C) + (m∠A + m∠B) \\\\\n&= 2m∠A + 2m∠B + 2m∠C \\\\\n&= 2(m∠A + m∠B + m∠C) \\\\\n&= 2(180^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 360^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3f605c56-a279-51cc-be43-72f1b9030f58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2226 :tutorial/title "The Sum of Exterior Angles"}]}
